      "updated": 1788694344063,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "High-Level Arrays Waste Space with \"Pointers\": Standard arrays and objects don't just hold your values—they store extra metadata, type\ntags, and memory addresses (pointers). The CPU has to jump to different places in memory just to collect the data, which wastes both\nRAM and processing cycles.\n\nWhy Heavy Tasks Crawl: For simple web forms, that pointer hopping doesn't matter. But for heavy workloads like streaming audio chunks\nfrom a TTS model, running games at 60 FPS, or processing image pixels chasing millions of scattered pointers creates lag, CPU cache\nmisses, and garbage collection freezes.\n\nThese Array buffer hold contiguous memory of only numbers (bits and bytes). You decide what those numbers mean—whether you run them\nthrough a text decoder for words, pass them to a sound card as audio waves, or send them to a GPU as image pixels.\n\none main point is if we have to read values byte by byte a normal array cannot do that we have to use unit8Array",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "High-Level Arrays Waste Space with \"Pointers\": Standard arrays and objects don't just hold your values—they store extra metadata, type tags, and memory addresses (pointers). The CPU has to jump to different places in memory just to collect the data, which wastes both RAM and processing cycles.\n\nWhy Heavy Tasks Crawl: For simple web forms, that pointer hopping doesn't matter. But for heavy workloads like streaming audio chunks from a TTS model, running games at 60 FPS, or processing image pixels chasing millions of scattered pointers creates lag, CPU cache misses, and garbage collection freezes.\n\nThese Array buffer hold contiguous memory of only numbers (bits and bytes). You decide what those numbers mean—whether you run them through a text decoder for words, pass them to a sound card as audio waves, or send them to a GPU as image pixels.\n\none main point is if we have to read values byte by byte a normal array cannot do that we have to use unit8Array",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
