      "updated": 1790160864890,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "DataView is another view over an ArrayBuffer.But by this we can store data dynamically like we can say store\nsetUnit8(0 , 100) , setUnit16(1 , 500) now here we are saying in the same buffer add 100 starting with index 0\nand for second we are saying add 500 starting with index 1.\n\n- TypedArray :- \"Interpret this memory as a sequence of one particular numeric type.\"\n- DataView  \"Let me read/write different types at specific byte offsets, with control over byte order.\"\n\nHow memory work ? when we store 500 in a Arraybuffer(4) (store 4 bytes of data each bytes have 8 bit of\nspace). now one byte can store 0 to 255 numbers (256 numbers).\nso , we do 500 / 256 = 1 quentiont and we get 244 as reminder.\nso for 1st bit we get 244 (in binary) and for second bit we get 1 (in binary). and when we print the buffer we\nget values in hex which is F4 01. F4 means 244 and 01 means 1 \nwhen we do \" const view16 = new Uint16Array(buffer); console.log(view16[0]);\" we get 500. now the default\nEndianness layout is little-endian.\n\nEndianness\n\nSuppose we have two bytes: 01 F4 How do we know whether that means: 500 or something else? Because\ncomputers need to know which byte comes first when storing multi-byte numbers. There are two common ways:-\n\n- Big-endian :- Most significant byte first: 01 F4\n- Little-endian:- Least significant byte first: F4 01\n\nnow when we are setting data at a buffer. we also have a option to choose in which layout we have to set the\ndata like view.setUint16(0, 500, true); The third argument: true means: Use little-endian. and for false use Big-\nendian and when we are reading the data we do view.getUint16(0, true) true means little-endian vice verca.\n\nWhy does this matter in real life? Because binary formats and network protocols have specific layouts. If you\ninterpret it using the wrong byte order, you'll get a completely different number.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "DataView is another view over an ArrayBuffer.But by this we can store data dynamically like we can say store setUnit8(0 , 100) , setUnit16(1 , 500) now here we are saying in the same buffer add 100 starting with index 0 and for second we are saying add 500 starting with index 1.\n\n- TypedArray :- \"Interpret this memory as a sequence of one particular numeric type.\"\n- DataView  \"Let me read/write different types at specific byte offsets, with control over byte order.\"\n\nHow memory work ? when we store 500 in a Arraybuffer(4) (store 4 bytes of data each bytes have 8 bit of space). now one byte can store 0 to 255 numbers (256 numbers).\nso , we do 500 / 256 = 1 quentiont and we get 244 as reminder.\nso for 1st bit we get 244 (in binary) and for second bit we get 1 (in binary). and when we print the buffer we get values in hex which is F4 01. F4 means 244 and 01 means 1 \nwhen we do \" const view16 = new Uint16Array(buffer); console.log(view16[0]);\" we get 500. now the default Endianness layout is little-endian.\n\nEndianness\n\nSuppose we have two bytes: 01 F4 How do we know whether that means: 500 or something else? Because computers need to know which byte comes first when storing multi-byte numbers. There are two common ways:-\n\n- Big-endian :- Most significant byte first: 01 F4\n- Little-endian:- Least significant byte first: F4 01\n\nnow when we are setting data at a buffer. we also have a option to choose in which layout we have to set the data like view.setUint16(0, 500, true); The third argument: true means: Use little-endian. and for false use Big-endian and when we are reading the data we do view.getUint16(0, true) true means little-endian vice verca.\n\nWhy does this matter in real life? Because binary formats and network protocols have specific layouts. If you interpret it using the wrong byte order, you'll get a completely different number.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
