      "updated": 1788593731039,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An object is essentially a collection of properties. Properties has key → value\n\nPrototype\nEvery ordinary JavaScript object has an internal link to another object called its prototype.\nObjects can inherit properties and methods from other objects. \n\nQ.) if we write obj.toString() how object get this property ?\nIf the property isn't found on the object itself, JavaScript looks up the object's [[Prototype]]\nand continues searching there.\n\nQ.) where does person's prototype come from?\nJs has Object.prototype and it basically attach it with objects's [[prototype]] and you can also\nsee the prototype by Object.getPrototypeOf(obj) you cannot do person.[[Prototype]] =\nObject.prototype; // ❌ because [[Prototype]] is an internal slot, not a normal JavaScript\nproperty.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "An object is essentially a collection of properties. Properties has key → value\n\nPrototype\nEvery ordinary JavaScript object has an internal link to another object called its prototype. Objects can inherit properties and methods from other objects. \n\nQ.) if we write obj.toString() how object get this property ?\nIf the property isn't found on the object itself, JavaScript looks up the object's [[Prototype]] and continues searching there.\n\nQ.) where does person's prototype come from?\nJs has Object.prototype and it basically attach it with objects's [[prototype]] and you can also see the prototype by Object.getPrototypeOf(obj) you cannot do person.[[Prototype]] = Object.prototype; // ❌ because [[Prototype]] is an internal slot, not a normal JavaScript property.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
