      "updated": 1790151491282,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When JavaScript normally handles data: for e.g const name = \"Shivam\" , const nums = [10, 20, 30]\nwe are mostly dealing with higher-level values. But computers ultimately deal with: 0 1 0 1 1 0 1 0\n... So JavaScript needs a way to work with raw binary memory. That's where ArrayBuffer comes\nin.\nIt is a fixed-size block of raw binary memory. const buffer = new ArrayBuffer(8); ( This asks\nJavaScript for 8 bytes of memory )",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When JavaScript normally handles data: for e.g const name = \"Shivam\" , const nums = [10, 20, 30] we are mostly dealing with higher-level values. But computers ultimately deal with: 0 1 0 1 1 0 1 0 ... So JavaScript needs a way to work with raw binary memory. That's where ArrayBuffer comes in.\nIt is a fixed-size block of raw binary memory. const buffer = new ArrayBuffer(8); ( This asks JavaScript for 8 bytes of memory )",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "xaEcidOPXHJz9lauNyuo7",
