    {
      "id": "z8Ed_1oqaUkBDPlozlWIN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 19180.291612692778,
      "y": -2079.587815864664,
      "width": 1784.7557638714359,
      "height": 591.4999999999993,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0Q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1609176326,
      "version": 7587,
      "versionNonce": 2042604018,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "95Z3emARVaFShZ4WHQPNS",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790151390880,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q.) If Object.create(anotherObject) creates a new object whose [[Prototype]] is anotherObject,\nthen is a class also an object ?\n\nA class in JavaScript is actually a special type of function (and functions are objects). now very\nfunction have a property \".prototype\" and it links to the object stored inside the class's .prototype\nproperty.\nAnd Object.create(anotherObject) is like extending a class when we do that we also get the\nproperty of parent class into the child class And now first understand the differece between two\nthing .prototype and [[prototype]]\n\n.prototype get the methods of the child class (or the normal class) and then its [[porototype]]\nhave the methods of parent class and this [[porototype]] have its [[porototype]] which have the\nObject.prototype methods and property ( for e.g. .toString() or etc.. ).",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q.) If Object.create(anotherObject) creates a new object whose [[Prototype]] is anotherObject, then is a class also an object ?\n\nA class in JavaScript is actually a special type of function (and functions are objects). now very function have a property \".prototype\" and it links to the object stored inside the class's .prototype property.\nAnd Object.create(anotherObject) is like extending a class when we do that we also get the property of parent class into the child class And now first understand the differece between two thing .prototype and [[prototype]]\n\n.prototype get the methods of the child class (or the normal class) and then its [[porototype]] have the methods of parent class and this [[porototype]] have its [[porototype]] which have the Object.prototype methods and property ( for e.g. .toString() or etc.. ).",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
