      "versionNonce": 1350036722,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "95Z3emARVaFShZ4WHQPNS",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790151686398,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Typed array cannot create space for raw bytes it is work of ArrayBuffer\n\nWhen you pass a buffer: new Uint8Array(buffer)\n- You already have an ArrayBuffer living in memory. The Uint8Array does not allocate new memory\nfor data. It simply acts as a pointer/window looking directly at your existing buffer.\n\nWhen you don't pass a buffer: new Uint8Array(4) or new Uint8Array([72, 101, 108])\n- The typed array still cannot hold raw bytes directly. Instead, JavaScript automatically create a\nbrand new, hidden ArrayBuffer behind the scenes The Uint8Array points to that newly created\nbuffer.\n\nWhich means ArrayBuffer create space in memory and TypedArray is used to add data in that\nmemory\n\nwhen you print a ArrayBuffer which have some data like at [0] = 100 and [1] = 200 and all other\nspace is filled with only 0's\nso it look like this <64 c8 00 00 00 00 00 00>. Why ?\nComputers commonly display raw bytes in hexadecimal (base 16). now again why hexadecimal ?\nbecause programmers love hexadecimal Bcz One byte has 8 bits: 11111111 That's annoying to read.\nBut in hex: FF Much easier.\n\n1 byte = 8 bits = 2 hexadecimal digits\nThat's why raw memory is so commonly displayed like: 00 FF A4 7B 12 C8 ...",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Typed array cannot create space for raw bytes it is work of ArrayBuffer\n\nWhen you pass a buffer: new Uint8Array(buffer)\n- You already have an ArrayBuffer living in memory. The Uint8Array does not allocate new memory for data. It simply acts as a pointer/window looking directly at your existing buffer.\n\nWhen you don't pass a buffer: new Uint8Array(4) or new Uint8Array([72, 101, 108])\n- The typed array still cannot hold raw bytes directly. Instead, JavaScript automatically create a brand new, hidden ArrayBuffer behind the scenes The Uint8Array points to that newly created buffer.\n\nWhich means ArrayBuffer create space in memory and TypedArray is used to add data in that memory\n\nwhen you print a ArrayBuffer which have some data like at [0] = 100 and [1] = 200 and all other space is filled with only 0's\nso it look like this <64 c8 00 00 00 00 00 00>. Why ?\nComputers commonly display raw bytes in hexadecimal (base 16). now again why hexadecimal ? because programmers love hexadecimal Bcz One byte has 8 bits: 11111111 That's annoying to read.\nBut in hex: FF Much easier.\n\n1 byte = 8 bits = 2 hexadecimal digits\nThat's why raw memory is so commonly displayed like: 00 FF A4 7B 12 C8 ...",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
